<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Halfdan the Black</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Halfdan Svarte · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ynglings">Ynglings</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Halfdan the Black was a king of Vestfold in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, known only from later sagas, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson">Snorri Sturluson</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heimskringla">Heimskringla</a>. The son of Gudrød the Hunter and Åsa of Agder, he is said to have extended his rule over Hadeland, Raumarike, Hedmark and other inland districts before drowning when his sleigh broke through ice. He was the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a>, traditionally the first king of all Norway.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/halfdan-the-black.jpg" alt="Halfdan the Black" width="320" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerhard Munthe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Halvdan_Svartes_saga-Tittelfrise-Gerhard_Munthe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Íslendingabók, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heimskringla">Heimskringla</a> and Fagrskinna make Halfdan a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ynglings">House of Yngling</a>, and Heimskringla names his half-brother Olaf Geirstad-Alf. When his father was killed, his mother Åsa took the one-year-old Halfdan back to Agder, where he was raised, and at 18 or 19 he became king there. Some scholars suggest that his descent from the earlier Yngling kings of Vestfold was a later invention, made to link a conquering Halfdan and his son with the family praised in the Ynglingatal, whom he had displaced.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Halfdan is said to have divided Vestfold with his brother Olaf and forced King Gandalf of Vingulmark to cede half his kingdom. In Raumarike he killed the ruler Sigtryg Eysteinsson in battle and then defeated Sigtryg's brother Eystein, gaining half of Hedmark as well; these details appear only in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heimskringla">Heimskringla</a>. His first wife Ragnhild was a daughter of King Harald Gulskeg of Sogn, and when their young son, who had been named his grandfather's heir, died, Halfdan claimed Sogn without resistance.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heimskringla">Heimskringla</a> adds that the sons of Gandalf tried to ambush him at night; he escaped into the forest, returned with an army, killed Hysing and Helsing and became king of all Vingulmark. The same saga says his second wife, also named Ragnhild, had been carried off by a berserker named Hake before Halfdan had her taken from him. Both sagas agree that she bore him a son named Harald.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heimskringla">Heimskringla</a>, Fagrskinna, Ágrip and Historia Norwegiæ all relate that he drowned at the inlet of Røykenvik in the lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/randsfjorden">Randsfjorden</a> on his way home from Hadeland, where ice had been weakened by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> dung near a watering hole. He was buried in a mound at Stein in Ringerike. Heimskringla adds that each district of his kingdom wanted his grave, so his body was divided into four, giving four sites called Halvdanshaugen; by this account only his head lies in Ringerike.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Halfdan the Black” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The sagas link it with the Vestfold dynasty through Queen Åsa, daughter of a king of Agder and mother of <b>Halfdan the Black</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/agder">Agder</a></li><li><blockquote>Snorri Sturluson's sagas say it was won by <b>Halfdan the Black</b>, father of Harald Fairhair, in the 9th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/hadeland">Hadeland</a></li><li><blockquote>The sagas say <b>Halfdan the Black of Vestfold</b> gained it by war and inheritance in the 9th century, making it part of the base from which Harald Fairhair built his kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/hedmark">Hedmark</a></li><li><blockquote>It is known only from the sagas, which describe it as a small kingdom taken by <b>Halfdan the Black of Vestfold</b> in the 9th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/raumarike">Raumarike</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the sagas, <b>Halfdan the Black</b> extended Vestfold's power over the Upplands, and his son Harald Fairhair conquered western Norway, traditionally at Hafrsfjord around 872.</blockquote><a href="/state/vestfold">Vestfold</a></li><li><blockquote>The sagas say <b>Halfdan the Black</b> won Vingulmark and that Harald Fairhair held it as part of his realm.</blockquote><a href="/state/vingulmark">Vingulmark</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Vestfold. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/randsfjorden">Randsfjorden</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=60.39,10.39&amp;year=864" aria-label="Randsfjorden on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Halfdan the Black"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5437 4932h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5437 4932h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=60.39,10.39&amp;year=864">What was here in 864 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
